   114	the line. Let `Bbar` be the projective closure. The normalization of
   115	`Bbar` is the smooth compactification of `A1`, namely `P1`, and the places
   116	of `B` at infinity are the points of `P1` over `Bbar ∩ L_infinity`, i.e.
   117	`P1 - A1`, a single point. Components of the link at infinity
   118	`B ∩ S^3_R` correspond to branches of `Bbar` at `L_infinity`, hence
   119	`K_infinity` is a knot. **CONFIRMED.**
   120	
   121	Side consequence used later: one place at infinity forces `B` irreducible
   122	(every affine curve component has at least one end), so the irreducibility
   123	hypothesis in (0.1) is redundant given the normalization hypothesis, and
   124	`f` is irreducible hence primitive (if `f = P(g)` with `deg P >= 2`, then
   125	`f` factors over `C`), so generic fibres are irreducible. The report does
   126	not use primitivity explicitly; it does not need to (Section 3.2).
   127	
   128	### 2.2 Corrected Neumann--Rudolph Lemma 7.1: CONFIRMED VERBATIM
   129	
   130	I obtained the corrigendum itself (Math. Ann. 282 (1988) 349-351,
   131	Gottinger Digitalisierungszentrum scan, `PPN235181684_0282/LOG_0040`) and
   132	read pages 349-351. The corrected statement is, literally:
   133	
   134	> **7.1. Lemma (A Knot at Infinity is Good).** If `V ⊂ C^2` is a fiber of
   135	> `f: C^2 -> C` and is *reduced* and its link at infinity is a knot (`V` is
   136	> connected at infinity) then `f` is good.
   137	
   138	Audit of every hypothesis against the report's use:
   139	
   140	```text
   141	"V is a fiber of f"          : V = f^{-1}(0) = B.                       OK
   142	"V is reduced"               : f squarefree, chosen so in Sec.2.        OK
   143	"link at infinity is a knot" : Section 2.1 above.                       OK
   144	NO smoothness/regularity hypothesis on V.                               OK
   145	NO primitivity hypothesis.                                              OK
   146	NO degree or genus hypothesis.                                          OK
   147	```
   148	
   149	This is the audit item that mattered most: the report applies 7.1 to a
   150	**singular** fibre (the one carrying the node). The corrected lemma places
   151	no non-singularity hypothesis on `V`, so the application is legitimate.
   152	The direction is also as stated (knot at infinity `=>` good, not the
   153	converse). **CONFIRMED.**
   154	
   155	The corrigendum's own definition, which is the one consumed:
   156	
   157	> The fiber `f^{-1}(c)` ... is *regular at infinity* if there exists a
   158	> neighborhood `D` of `c` and a compact set `K` such that
   159	> `f | f^{-1}(D) - K : f^{-1}(D) - K -> D` is a locally trivial `C^inf`
   160	> fibration. The polynomial map `f` is *good* if every fiber is regular at
   161	> infinity. ... We denote by `H(f,inf)` the link at infinity of any fiber
   162	> which is regular at infinity; up to isotopy this is independent of the
   163	> choice of the fiber.
   164	
   165	That last clause is exactly the report's "goodness identifies the links at
   166	infinity of `f^{-1}(0)` and `f^{-1}(epsilon)`". **CONFIRMED.**
   167	
   168	The report's three claims about the corrigendum are each true:
   169	
   170	```text
   171	"corrects the definition of a good polynomial"  -> p.349: "the definition
   172	     of 'good' must be modified as follows", replacing the old
   173	     "only isolated singularities" definition.                        OK
   174	"explicitly restates and reproves the lemma"    -> p.349 restatement,
   175	     p.351 "Proof of 7.1".                                            OK
   176	"Neumann 1989, page 446, consumes the corrected form" -> Neumann 1989
   177	     p.445-446 cites "[N-R 1, Lemma 7.1]", and the 1989 bibliography
   178	     defines [N-R 1] as the 1987 paper "and: Corrigendum ... ibid. 282,
   179	     349-351 (1988)". The citation therefore points at the corrected
   180	     lemma.                                                           OK
   181	```
   182	
   183	Neumann 1989, pp. 445-446, states the consequence in the report's own
   184	words: `f` is good "if any fiber of `f` is reduced and has a knot as its
   185	link at infinity (i.e., it is connected at infinity), cf. [N-R 1, Lemma 7.1]".
   186	
   187	The corrigendum's proof of 7.1 also gives a bonus the report does not use:
   188	"By Suzuki [S], the general fiber of `f` is also connected at infinity, so
   189	`H(f,inf)` is a knot. As described in [N-R], **it is an iterated torus
   190	knot**, hence fiberable, so `f` is good by Theorem 6.1." See Section 4.2.
   191	
   192	Note the corrigendum's Example on p.349, `f = x^2 y + x`, whose zero fibre
   193	has three components at infinity while nearby fibres have two: this is the
   194	canonical failure of goodness, and it has no knot fibre, consistent with
   195	7.1. I re-derived that example independently before reading the page.
   196	
   197	## 3. Obligation 2 -- the nearby fibre and the genus-one core
   198	
   199	### 3.1 Node smoothing and absence of hidden vanishing cycles: CONFIRMED
   200	
   201	Given goodness, choose `R >> 0` with `f` transverse to `S^3_R` over a small
   202	disc `D` about 0 containing no other critical value, and a Milnor ball `M`
   203	about the sole node. Over `D`, `f` restricted to `B_R - M` is proper with
   204	no critical point, and by goodness the exterior of `B_R` is a product; so
   205	Ehresmann gives triviality outside `M`, and the only change from
   206	`f^{-1}(0)` to `f^{-1}(epsilon)` is replacement of the node cone by the
   207	node Milnor fibre. Absence of vanishing cycles at infinity is exactly the
   208	"regular at infinity" clause; absence of other affine vanishing cycles is
   209	hypothesis (0.1). **CONFIRMED.**
   210	
   211	### 3.2 Euler count, connectedness, genus: CONFIRMED
   212	
   213	Core of the normalization is a disc, `chi = 1`; delete two discs at the two
   214	node preimages (an ordinary node has two distinct smooth branches, so the
   215	preimages are distinct), `chi = -1`, three boundary circles; glue the
   216	node Milnor fibre, an annulus with `chi = 0`, along two of them:
   217	`chi(A ∪_{S^1 ⊔ S^1} B) = chi(A) + chi(B) = -1`, one boundary circle left,
   218	which is `K_infinity`. Then `2 - 2g - 1 = -1` gives `g = 1`. **CONFIRMED.**
   219	
